  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalling cold front may bring rain at times\nthrough Sunday.\n\nExtensive cloud cover remains over much of the region, although\nsome cloud breaks are noted along I-70 in north-central\nMaryland. The array of nearby radars are showing a band of more\nconcentrated rain tracking just east of the Blue Ridge toward\nthe I-95 metros. This will make for a rainy morning amidst the\ncool post-frontal air mass. The stable nature of the air mass is\nlargely limiting hourly amounts to around a trace to 0.05 \ninches. The 12Z IAD sounding did show some dry air in the surface\nto 900 mb layer. Despite precipitable water values of 1.55\ninches, this drier low-level air is eroding some of this\nrainfall before reaching the ground. Elsewhere, dry air will \nwork into the region from the north and northwest behind the\nearlier cold front.\n\nStrong high pressure will pass north of the area today while \nthe front stalls just to our south. Southern stream moisture \nwill likely overrun the boundary fairly far to the north, but \nthere remains some uncertainty in the dividing line between rain\nand no rain. The trend has been for a further north track, so \nhave shifted rain chances about 20 to 30 miles north with the \nlatest forecast package. \n\nA wave of low pressure will ride along this stalled boundary \ntonight into Sunday, and this may provide a higher chance of \nsome heavier rain spreading northward into the area. The Weather\nPrediction Center does have our eastern areas in a Marginal \nExcessive Rainfall Outlook, and this seems plausible given the \npotential for a surge of 2\"+ PWATs. Rain that does occur will \nlikely exit the area Sunday evening.\n\nBeing north of the boundary and potentially having a lot of\ncloud cover, high temperatures will likely remain in the 60s for\nmost areas over the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...First widespread frost and freeze potential west of \nthe Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nSignificant pattern change for the first full week of Oct. CAA \nbehind a departing trof will bring the coldest air of the fall thus \nfar to the region. A robust area of Canadian high pressure moves \ninto the area Mon night through mid-week. Consequently, this is \nforecast to bring a true autumnal feel to the air with daily temps \nwell below average. For Tue and Wed, forecast highs will generally \nrange from the upper 50s to 60s, with mountain locales mainly in the \n50s. Looking at overnight conditions, the dry nature of the air mass \nallows temperatures to plummet at night. Areas west of the Blue \nRidge can expect widespread mid/upper 30s on Mon and Tue nights. \nWould not be surprised if some areas just east of the Blue Ridge or \nin northern Maryland have frost as well. This will also include some \npockets of upper 20s to near the freezing mark. Given the growing \nseason continues across the region, there will be a threat of some \nfrost/freeze conditions both of those nights. For those with \nsensitive outdoor vegetation, plans should be made to ensure their \nsafety in these conditions.\n\nAlthough Canadian high pressure moves off the coast toward the \nmid/late week period, the overall pattern will remain dry throughout \nnext week. Temps slowly rebound through the week as forecast highs \nreturn to the low/mid 70s. Nighttime lows remain chilly, but the \nthreat for frost/freeze conditions should diminish beyond Wed \nmorning.",
